  {
    "episode_id": 74167,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/4/74167.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 25,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, I think, you know, the sociologist can study this, but in my own experience, it was very difficult and a lot of ways for survival. It was a, okay, I'm going to be one of the guys. And if I didn't like the way the other woman behaved, then she was making me look bad. And so we ended up not working together. And again, it wasn't necessarily a conscious thing. I really think it is helpful for people to have wingmen and wingwomen. And I learned that. when I was in my A-10 squadron and another female A-10 pilot showed up in my squadron. And actually, we meet the bullet, it was her name. And 23 years ago tonight, she died in the A-10 crash on the very Goldwater Range here in Arizona. And I was just north of her, and she was on the southern mission. I was on the North Tech mission when when she crashed and we weren't sure if she ejected and we didn't know until the next morning that she didn't survive. But when she first came into my squadron, I was a little concerned. Was she going to measure up? Was she going to be, you know, carry herself well and professionally? It's just silly, but when you're in survival mode, this tends to be what happens. but she was such a breath of fresh air. She showed that the environment had changed and the squadron treated her with respect and dignity and I hopefully laid the groundwork for that and she and I could work together in the banter and you know and the back and forth that happens in fighter squadrons and it was great to have a wingwoman there with me and it obviously was so devastating when we lost her and her family is in my prayers today I talked to them this weekend.",
