  {
    "episode_id": 74167,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/4/74167.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 33,
    "turn_text": "That scene is in Saudi Arabia and it's in the middle of an eight-year battle that I have with the Pentagon where they were making our service woman in Saudi Arabia be treated as if they were a local Saudi woman in their seventh century norms. So that we had to wear a taxpayer funded essentially a burqa with black, full gown, head, black head scar if your face was open, be treated like second class citizens. And this is while you're serving. And this didn't apply to me for the first five years that I was fighting this. But one of my lessons in the book was never walked by a problem. And I walked by a picture of a young enlisted female troop in Saudi Arabia wearing that abaya and I just had this conviction that it was wrong. And I struggled with whether I should stand up and put my career on the line in order to fight for this. But I share a story of the story of Esther. in the Old Testament, and the line, can it be that you were put in this position for such a time as this? And so I never would have imagined it would be an eight-year battle. It culminated in me suing Donald Rumsfeld, McSally versus Rumsfeld, and then on a one-woman lobbying campaign, getting legislation signed into law to overturn this. I was back in Saudi Arabia last year. I drove a car with jeans on. It was very cathartic for me many decades later. And it's a perfect case of bureaucratic policy creep in the military. It was a military doing it to itself, but they thought it was somebody else, and it was just a classic case. And so I share that example in order to encourage the reader, you may not have to sue the Secretary of Defense, but we should all have the mantra, don't walk by a problem. If you see something wrong, stop and do something about it. Do your part to fix it. This just took eight years, a lot of effort in putting my career on the line, and I would do it again in a heartbeat.",
